const book='https://jax-ml.github.io/scaling-book/inference/';
const wafer='https://github.com/wafer-ai/gpu-perf-engineering-resources';
const weeks=[
['Serving and benchmarking a small language model','CPU','Tokenization, decoder shapes, latency versus throughput. Read the opening of the inference chapter; map the infrastructure overview onto a request.','Build a hardware report, a SmolLM2-135M benchmark, and a weight / KV memory calculator. Start at batch 1 with 64, 128, and 256 prompt tokens.','Environment manifest, raw latency and tokens/s, peak RSS, and a diagram of the request path. Keep model loading separate.','What I can measure before optimizing inference. Publish a thread with the setup, baseline table, and next question.',book],
['Implementing the prefill and decode loop','CPU','Causal masking, next-token logits, positions, and stopping conditions. Revisit prefill and generation in the scaling book.','Replace .generate() with your own greedy loop using full-prefix recomputation. Implement a separate tiny decoder under 10M parameters using tensor operations.','Parity against reference greedy generation on 20 prompts. Test EOS, maximum length, and causal masking.','I replaced .generate() with a loop I can explain. Show the loop, shapes, and redundant work.',book],
['Building a KV cache','CPU','Cached keys and values, position offsets, and memory growth. Read the inference chapter alongside the cache-size calculation.','Add per-layer preallocated K/V buffers, prefill, single-token decode, reset, and capacity checks to the tiny decoder.','Cached / uncached logits within a stated tolerance; cache isolation tests; tensor bytes versus predicted memory; latency versus context.','Writing my own KV cache: what it saves and what it costs. Publish an 800–1,200-word article and a short thread.',book],
['Static batching: throughput and latency','CPU','Weight reuse, padding, and arithmetic intensity. Use the scaling book to predict how batch size changes the workload.','Add per-request lengths, masks, and stopping states. Compare batches 1, 2, and 4 with equal and mixed lengths.','Batched / individual correctness; throughput; per-request latency; padded versus useful work. Cap memory before increasing the batch.','Batching improved throughput—but what happened to each request? Report the real result, including a slowdown.',book],
['Continuous batching and request scheduling','CPU','Iteration-level scheduling and request lifecycles. Read continuous batching in the book and the Orca paper linked by Wafer.','Implement arrival, admission, completion, cancellation, and cache reclamation. Replay a fixed mixed-length trace with up to four active requests.','Static versus continuous throughput, queue delay, p50/p95 TTFT, inter-token latency, and cancellation/cache-isolation tests.','My first continuous-batching scheduler. Show a timeline, the scheduling policy, and one fairness limitation.',wafer],
['Inside a production inference engine','GPU*','Request admission, scheduling, KV allocation, and execution. Follow one request through a pinned vLLM source revision.','Build a replay adapter and annotated source map. With GPU access, compare two scheduler configurations on the same small-model workload.','Pinned source links, matched workload manifests, and measured serving results when GPU access is available.','Reading vLLM after building a tiny inference engine. Publish the source map and one concrete investigation.','https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/design/arch_overview/'],
['Quantization: smaller weights, different tradeoffs','CPU','Scales, clipping, outliers, and weight-only execution. Use the AWQ reference in Wafer to understand the design problem.','Implement per-channel INT8 linear weights with an explicit dequantizing reference path. Compare against FP32 on the same model.','Stored bytes, peak memory, numerical error, and latency. For pretrained quality checks, measure held-out text loss.','Smaller weights did not automatically make inference faster. Distinguish storage savings from low-bit execution.',wafer],
['Speculative decoding: drafting and verification','CPU','Drafting, parallel verification, acceptance, and rollback. Read the original speculative-decoding paper.','Implement greedy speculative decoding for a tiny target/draft pair. Sweep draft lengths 1, 2, and 4. Use larger pretrained pairs only if memory permits.','Exact greedy token parity, forced rejection at every position, EOS handling, accepted-prefix lengths, draft time, verify time, and speedup or slowdown.','I implemented speculative decoding—and measured when drafting pays off. State that greedy equivalence is the scope.','https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.17192'],
['Profiling an inference workload','GPU*','Host overhead, memory traffic, launches, and Amdahl’s law. Read Wafer’s Nsight profiling references.','Instrument prefill, decode, cache updates, and scheduling. Capture CPU profiles now; GPU timelines and counters require GPU access.','A time breakdown, one falsifiable hypothesis, a controlled intervention, and an upper bound on possible end-to-end improvement.','Where one generated token spends its time. Publish an annotated trace and explain what would disprove the hypothesis.',wafer],
['Writing a fused softmax kernel in Triton','GPU','Program IDs, vectorized loads, masks, reductions, and fusion. Work through the official fused-softmax tutorial.','Implement fused row-wise softmax. Compare against unfused tensor operations and torch.softmax; cover awkward widths and large input magnitudes.','Correctness tolerances, latency across shapes, effective bandwidth, and a separate end-to-end measurement if integrated. Keep tensors under 256 MiB.','My first Triton softmax: which shapes win, and which lose? GPU measurements remain pending until run.','https://triton-lang.org/main/getting-started/tutorials/02-fused-softmax.html'],
['CUDA fundamentals through memory access','GPU','Threads, blocks, coalescing, shared memory, and synchronization. Read the CUDA basics and matrix-transpose reference linked by Wafer.','Write naive and shared-memory tiled CUDA matrix transpose. Add boundary handling and investigate padding for bank conflicts.','Rectangular and non-tile-aligned correctness; memory checking; bandwidth against a device-copy baseline. GPU required; keep working tensors under 256 MiB.','Changing memory access patterns in my first CUDA kernel. Explain the access pattern with a diagram and measured results.',wafer],
['An end-to-end inference optimization case study','CPU / GPU','Serving evaluation and deployment constraints. Revisit the infrastructure overview and Wafer’s benchmark references.','Harden one earlier optimization. Add a memory limit, overload behavior, a runbook, and clean-checkout reproduction instructions.','Raw results, correctness checks, workload matrix, a regression case, and throughput meeting a predeclared latency SLO. GPU claims require actual GPU runs.','Building an inference engine: one optimization I can defend. Link the engine, performance study, and kernel work.',wafer]
];
const root=document.getElementById('weeks');
weeks.forEach((w,i)=>{const detail=document.createElement('details');detail.className='week';detail.id=`week-${i+1}`;const summary=document.createElement('summary');summary.innerHTML=`<span class="weeknum">${String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span><span class="weektitle">${w[0]}</span><span class="execution">${w[1]}<b aria-hidden="true">+</b></span>`;detail.append(summary);const body=document.createElement('div');body.className='week-body';['LEARN / READ','BUILD','CHECKABLE OUTPUT','PUBLISH'].forEach((label,j)=>{const section=document.createElement('div');const h=document.createElement('h4');h.textContent=label;const p=document.createElement('p');p.textContent=w[j+2];section.append(h,p);if(j===0){const a=document.createElement('a');a.href=w[6];a.textContent='Open the reading ↗';a.style.fontSize='14px';section.append(a)}body.append(section)});if(i===0){const link=document.createElement('a');link.href='lesson-01.html';link.className='text-link';link.textContent='Open the completed experiment and lesson ↗';body.append(link)}detail.append(body);root.append(detail)});
const hardware=document.createElement('p');hardware.className='section-meta';hardware.style.maxWidth='none';hardware.textContent='GPU* = CPU preparation is possible; GPU execution is still required for the performance study. The first lesson runs on the VM’s two CPU cores.';root.append(hardware);
function revealAnchor(){const id=decodeURIComponent(location.hash.slice(1));const target=document.getElementById(id);if(target?.tagName==='DETAILS'){target.open=true;requestAnimationFrame(()=>target.scrollIntoView({block:'start'}))}}
window.addEventListener('hashchange',revealAnchor);revealAnchor();
